sec.p:
To each of the four available options, indexed below by “o” (from 1 to 4, referring to Antifragile, Fragile, Robust, Vulnerable) in equations (1), we attach at each moment in trial/time t a gain sub-value Qg(o) and a loss sub-value Ql(o) that are updated as follows:

(1)

Qt+1g(o)−Qtg(o)⏟update of gain sub-value =αg⏟gain learning rate ×[rtg(o)−Qtg(o)]⏟gain prediction error Qt+1l(o)−Qtl(o)⏟update of loss sub-value =αl⏟loss learning rate ×[rtl(o)−Qtl(o)]⏟loss prediction error 
\begin{document}$$\begin{array}{ccccc}
\underbrace{Q_{t+1}^{g}(o)-Q_{t}^{g}(o)}_{\text {update of gain sub-value }} &=& \underbrace{\alpha_{g}}_{\text {gain learning rate }} & \times &\underbrace{\left[r_{t}^{g}(o)-Q_{t}^{g}(o)\right]}_{\text {gain prediction error }}
\\ \underbrace{Q_{t+1}^{l}(o)-Q_{t}^{l}(o)}_{\text {update of loss sub-value }} &=& \underbrace{\alpha_{l}}_{\text {loss learning rate }} & \times &\underbrace{\left[r_{t}^{l}(o)-Q_{t}^{l}(o)\right]}_{\text {loss prediction error }}\end{array}$$\end{document}



where the convention that updated values are indexed by t + 1 means right after observing the gain rtg\begin{document}$r_{t}^{g}$\end{document} or loss rtl\begin{document}$r_{t}^{l}$\end{document} corresponding to the choice made at t. Parameters αg and αl are usually labeled learning rates, indicating the speed at which reward prediction error gets updated into the latest values. Limiting cases occur when setting the learning rate either to zero - its smallest possible value - which means no subvalue updating, or to one - its largest possible value - which means that the subvalue tracks the obtained reward itself. While equations (1) applies for the updating of the chosen option, a similar updating rule applies for the three other options that are not chosen at that moment, by assuming that this happens as if the reward was zero, with different parameters. That is, for options that are not chosen, the updating rules for subvalues are simply Qt+1g(o)=(1−αgf)Qtg(o)\begin{document}$Q_{t+1}^{g}(o)=\left(1-\alpha_{g}^{f}\right) Q_{t}^{g}(o)$\end{document} and Qt+1l(o)=(1−αlf)Qtl(o)\begin{document}$Q_{t+1}^{l}(o)=\left(1-\alpha_{l}^{f}\right) Q_{t}^{l}(o)$\end{document}, where the forgetting rates αgf\begin{document}$\alpha_{g}^{f}$\end{document} and αlf\begin{document}$\alpha_{l}^{f}$\end{document} are assumed to be bounded between zero and one.

Making use of the exponential transform, we define Jensen gaps for the convex and concave exposures as, respectively, JGX=∑i=14piexiX−e∑i=14pixiX,JGV=∑i=14piexiV−e∑i=14pixiV\begin{document}$\mathcal{J G}_{\mathcal{X}}=\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i} e^{x_{i}^{\mathcal{X}}}-e^{\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i} x_{i}^{\mathcal{X}}}, \mathcal{J} \mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{V}}=\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i} e^{x_{i}^{\mathcal{V}}}-e^{\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i} x_{i}^{\mathcal{V}}}$\end{document} which relate to statistical moments as follows. The moment generating function for the convex and concave exposures are given by, respectively: MX(t)=∑i=14pietxiX\begin{document}$M_{\mathcal{X}}(t)=\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i} e^{t x_{i}^{\mathcal{X}}}$\end{document} and MV(t)=∑i=14pietxiV\begin{document}$M_{\mathcal{V}}(t)=\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i} e^{t x_{i}^{\mathcal{V}}}$\end{document}. The series expansion of the exponential function allows us to derive:
MX(1)=1+m1X+12!m2X+13!m3X+⋯+1N!mNX+…MV(1)=1+m1V+12!m2V+13!m3V+⋯+1N!mNV+… \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} M_{\mathcal{X}}(1) & =1+m_{1}^{\mathcal{X}}+\frac{1}{2!} m_{2}^{\mathcal{X}}+\frac{1}{3!} m_{3}^{\mathcal{X}}+\cdots+\frac{1}{N!} m_{N}^{\mathcal{X}}+\ldots \\ M_{\mathcal{V}}(1) & =1+m_{1}^{\mathcal{V}}+\frac{1}{2!} m_{2}^{\mathcal{V}}+\frac{1}{3!} m_{3}^{\mathcal{V}}+\cdots+\frac{1}{N!} m_{N}^{\mathcal{V}}+\ldots\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where mNX\begin{document}$m_{N}^{\mathcal{X}}$\end{document} and mNV\begin{document}$m_{N}^{\mathcal{V}}$\end{document} are the N-th statistical raw moments of the convex and concave exposures, respectively. For example,mNX=∑i=14pi(xiX)N\begin{document}$m_{N}^{\mathcal{X}}=\sum_{i=1}^{4} p_{i}\left(x_{i}^{\mathcal{X}}\right)^{N}$\end{document} for any integer N ≥ 1. Jensen’s gaps for both exposures are then given by JX=MX(1)−em1X,JGV=MV(1)−em1V\begin{document}$\mathcal{J}_{\mathcal{X}}=M_{\mathcal{X}}(1)-e^{m_{1}^{\mathcal{X}}}, \mathcal{J} \mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{V}}=M_{\mathcal{V}}(1)-e^{m_{1}^{\mathcal{V}}}$\end{document}. Using again the series expansion of the exponential of the first moment for each exposure, it follows that both positive Jensen gaps can be written in terms of moments, as follows:
(3)
JGX=∑k=2∞mkX−(m1X)kk!,JGV=∑k=2∞mkV−(m1V)kk! \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathcal{J} \mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{X}}=\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{m_{k}^{\mathcal{X}}-\left(m_{1}^{\mathcal{X}}\right)^{k}}{k!}, \quad \mathcal{J} \mathcal{G}_{\mathcal{V}}=\sum_{k=2}^{\infty} \frac{m_{k}^{\mathcal{V}}-\left(m_{1}^{\mathcal{V}}\right)^{k}}{k!} \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Note that the sums in equation (3) nicely allow for a straightforward decomposition in terms on all raw moments with order larger than two.

As a robustness check, we introduce in our augmented Q-learning models the range principle due to [36] - see also [34] - which captures the notion that subjective evaluation of rewards may take into account their range through the Min-Max normalization presented below. That is, we replace objective rewards by their subjective judgements s(.) as follows:


s(rk)=rk−rminkrmaxk−rminkwithk∈{g,l}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle s(r^k) = \frac{r^k - r^k_\text{min}}{r^k_\text{max} - r^k_\text{min}} \quad \text{with} \quad k \in \{g,l\}$$\end{document}



Subjective judgements were then substituted in equation (1) for gains and losses and the resulting Q-learning models were estimated using the procedure described in Material and Methods, section Augmented Q-Learning Model Estimation and Simulation. More precisely, subjective judgements for gains and losses are as follows: s(rg) = (rg − 1)/(80 − 1) for options that give convex gains and s(rg) = (rg − 2)/(5 − 2) for options that give concave gains; similarly, s(rl) = (rl + 15)/(−6 + 15) when losses are convex losses and s(rl) = (rl + 240)/(−3 + 240) when losses are concave. REE are then used (as Min and Max values) to normalize all rewards and they are included in the Q-sub-values when they happen. This implies, by definition, that subjective judgements equal zero when Black Swans materialize, and equal one when Jackpots occur.
